  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated showers linger through the evening, but\nthere will be no severe weather threat. \n\nThe primary cold front is currently nosing southward while\nmoving into northern sections of North Carolina. Despite the\npost-frontal air mass, humidity levels have not fully dropped\noff yet with current dew points in the mid 60s to low 70s.\nCompared the 00Z IAD sounding from the one this morning showed\nmuch of the drying occurring in the 700-mb and above layer. Looking\non radar, most afternoon activity has waned with the loss of \ndaytime heating. However, some residual isolated showers \npersist, mainly along I-64 and up toward the Shenandoah Valley. \nThese too should dissipate in time with diminishing levels of \ninstability. Low temperatures will drop several degrees from \nprevious nights into the 60s for most, even mid 50s in the \nmountains.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures and \nlower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic Sunday into Monday, with accompanying mid-level \ndry air that will bring down humidity and t-storm chances. Daily\nhighs drop into the low 80s, along with drier dewpoints in the \nmid to upper 50s. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid-Atlantic \non Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the Southeast. \nTemperatures likely begin to creep up again during this time, \naccompanied by increasing humidity as mid-level dry air tracks south \nof the region with more humid air coming in from the north. This \nwill correspond with strong upper-level trough forming over Quebec \naround midweek, with an accompanying cold front tracking across the \nEast Coast sometime between Wednesday and Thursday. This could \ncreate some chances for precipitation over the region, with some \nindicators for low-end instability over the area as this mid-level \nmoisture tracks southeast. Severe probabilities look low at this \ntime, but a few thunderstorms are not completely out of the question \nas this front moves through.\n\nLooking towards late next week, temperatures likely drop again in \nthe wake of this cold front Friday as it tracks to the south.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Shower and thunderstorm chances return by mid\nweek.\n\nEntering into the later half of next week, southerly return flow \nfrom a high pressure system just off the Mid-Atlatic coast drives \nwarm, moist air into our region. Models depict a surface low \ndeepening over Quebec and advancing a corresponding cold front \nthrough our region sometime late next week. There is still a quite a \nbit of uncertainty associated with the arrival of this boundary to \nour region, with its arrival ranging between Wednesday and Thursday. \nA bove-normal high temperatures and dew points out ahead of this \nboundary could potentially destabilize the atmosphere and lead to \nshowers and thunderstorms late next week.",
